] "\n" [:option {:value "255796"} "Kayak Singles, 1,000 metres, Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "255809"} "Kayak Doubles, 500 metres, Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "255822"} "Kayak Doubles, 1,000 metres, Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "255835"} "Kayak Fours, 1,000 metres, Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "255848"} "Canadian Singles, 500 metres, Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "255861"} "Canadian Singles, 1,000 metres, Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "255873"} "Canadian Doubles, 500 metres, Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "255897"} "Kayak Singles, 500 metres, Women"] "\n" [:option {:value "255907"} "Kayak Doubles, 500 metres, Women"] "\n" [:option {:value "255919"} "Kayak Fours, 500 metres, Women"]] [:script {} "$('#same_games_select').change(function(){location.href='/results/' + $('#same_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:form {:class "form-inline form-inline-header"} [:select {:name "id", :id "other_games_select", :class "form-control"} [:option {:value ""} "In other Games"] "\n" [:option {:value "255017"} "1936"] "\n" [:option {:value "255043"} "1948"] "\n" [:option {:value "255074"} "1952"] "\n" [:option {:value "255107"} "1956"] "\n" [:option {:value "255203"} "1960"] "\n" [:option {:value "255270"} "1964"] "\n" [:option {:value "255334"} "1968"] "\n" [:option {:value "255399"} "1972"] "\n" [:option {:value "255545"} "1976"] "\n" [:option {:value "255657"} "1980"] "\n" [:option {:value "255763"} "1984"] "\n" [:option {:value "256022"} "1992"] "\n" [:option {:value "256152"} "1996"] "\n" [:option {:value "256265"} "2000"] "\n" [:option {:value "256382"} "2004"] "\n" [:option {:value "258809"} "2008"] "\n" [:option {:value "307107"} "2012"] "\n" [:option {:value "353532"} "2016"] "\n" [:option {:value "19016188"} "2020"]] [:script {} "$('#other_games_select').change(function(){location.href='/results/' + $('#other_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:a {:href "/event_names/172"} "Event type"]] [:h1 {:class "event_title"} "Canadian Doubles, 1,000 metres, Men" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "27 September –  1 October 1988"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/640"} "Mi-sa-ri Jo-jeong-gyeong-gi-jang, Hanam"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/255886"} "34 from 17 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "Results for the C-2 1000 at the last three World Championships had been inconsistent, but Poland’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/10471"} "Marek Dopierała"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/10502"} "Marek Łbik"] " had medalled in every one. The only World Champion from the last three years to attend the 1988 Summer Olympics was East Germany’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/9922"} "Olaf Heukrodt"] ", the 1985 victor, who would become an Olympic champion in the C-1 500 in Seoul. For this event he was partnered with " [:a {:href "/athletes/9985"} "Ingo Spelly"] ". The other two World Championship nations, Hungary and the Soviet Union, sent " [:a {:href "/athletes/10105"} "Gábor Takács"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/10079"} "Gusztáv Leikep"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/11007"} "Victor Reneischi"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/11026"} "Nicolae Juravschi"] " respectively. 1984 Olympic C-1 500 champions and C-1 1000 runners-up " [:a {:href "/athletes/11205"} "Matija Ljubek"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/11209"} "Mirko Nišović"] " were also present, as was 1984 Olympic C-1 1000 bronze medalist " [:a {:href "/athletes/9638"} "Didier Hoyer"] " of France, who was partnered with " [:a {:href "/athletes/9675"} "Pascal Sylvoz"] "."] "\n\n" [:p {} "East Germany posted the fastest time and defeated both Poland and Hungary in the opening round, all of whom helped relegate Bulgaria to the repêchage, while France came in second to Romania’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/10602"} "Grigore Obreja"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/10570"} "Gheorghe Andriev"] " and the Soviet Union won a third heat. The semi-finals, meanwhile, were won by the Soviet Union, Poland, and East Germany, while Yugoslavia was eliminated in the latter’s heat.  Reneischi and Juravschi dominated the final, winning their second Olympic title of the Games (their first had been in the C-2 500) by a margin of over three seconds. Similarly, East Germany was able to take silver by just under three seconds, but Poland had to battle the Danish duo of " [:a {:href "/athletes/9464"} "Christian Frederiksen"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/9451"} "Arne Nielsson"] " to claim bronze, which it did by a margin of 0.61 seconds. It was their second medal of the tournament as well, as they earned silver in the C-2 500 the day before."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Pair"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "Rep"] [:th {} "SF"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "396/394"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/11007"} "Victor Reneischi"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/11026"} "Nicolae Juravschi"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/URS"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/URS.png"}] "URS"]] [:td {} "3:48.27 (1 h1)"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "3:46.85 (1 h1)"] [:td {} "3:48.36 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {:class "bib"} "252/256"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/9922"} "Olaf Heukrodt"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/9985"} "Ingo Spelly"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GDR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GDR.png"}] "GDR"]] [:td {} "3:40.82 (1 h3)"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "3:50.10 (1 h3)"] [:td {} "3:51.44 (2)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "3"] [:td {:class "bib"} "342/343"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athle